{"cuisine/mauritian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/mauritian-cuisine","n":"Mauritian cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Mauritius","intro":[["The cuisine of Mauritius reflects the island’s tropical environment and its long history of cultural diversity. Mauritian food traditions incorporate elements of African cuisine, Chinese cuisine, European cuisine—particularly French cuisine—and Indian cuisine, especially Bhojpuri cuisine, reflecting the various groups that settled on the island."],["Dishes and culinary customs developed through the interactions of different groups on the island, including those of French settlers, African slaves, Indian indentured labourers, and Chinese migrants in the 19th century. Over time, these communities incorporated and adapted each other's food practices, contributing to the formation of Mauritian cuisine."],["Some dishes are widely consumed across ethnic groups, while others remain associated with particular communities for cultural or historical reasons. Regional and community-based culinary traditions include Indo-Mauritian cuisine, Creole cuisine, Sino-Mauritian cuisine, and Franco-Mauritian cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"mauritian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauritian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Thali","dish/thali"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Foodwini","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"foodwini.com","note":"Mauritius based food blog by Hanshini, leaning vegetarian, with many Mauritian recipes.","url":"https://foodwini.com/about-foodwini-mauritius-food-blog/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"The Island Kitchen: Recipes from Mauritius and the Indian Ocean","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Selina Periampillai, 2019","note":"Mauritian and wider Indian Ocean island recipes from a London based cook of Mauritian heritage.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781526601384","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781526601384"},{"name":"Sunshine on a Plate","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Shelina Permalloo, 2013","note":"Mauritian home cooking from the 2012 MasterChef UK winner.","url":"https://thehappyfoodie.co.uk/?p=10406","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780091950811"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Groat","ingredient/groat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"African cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/3/38/Mauritian_Dalpuri.jpg/500px-Mauritian_Dalpuri.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Unknown authorUnknown author","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mauritian_Dalpuri.jpg"}},"cuisine/arab-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/arab-cuisine","n":"Arab cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Malaysian cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Malaysia","intro":[["Arab cuisine refers to the regional culinary traditions of the Arab world, spanning the Maghreb (the west) and the Mashriq (the east). These cuisines are centuries old and reflect Arab trade in ingredients, spices, herbs, and commodities. The regions share many similarities, but also have unique traditions. Climate, cultivation, and commerce have influenced them."],["Across the region, staple ingredients include wheat and rice, lamb and chicken, yoghurt and other dairy products, and a wide range of legumes and vegetables, seasoned with herbs and spices such as cumin, cinnamon, saffron, and sumac. Coffee and tea, often flavored with cardamom, are popular beverages along with arak in some places."],["Hospitality and generosity towards guests are central to Arab food culture, typically shared through large shared meals, with regional customs dictating how guests are welcomed and received. During Ramadan, people break their daily fast each evening with iftar, traditionally with dates."],["Regional cuisine varies considerably. Levantine cuisine is known for olive oil, za'atar, and mezze dishes such as hummus and falafel; Iraqi cuisine, documented in what is considered the oldest surviving food traditions, is associated with dishes such as masgouf. Egyptian cuisine centres on largely vegetarian staples including ful medames and Koshari; the Gulf and Arabian Peninsula have dishes such as kabsa and mandi. Yemeni food is distinct from neighbors, known for dishes such as saltah. Bedouin cuisine relies heavily on dates, dried fruits, and preserves suited to a nomadic lifestyle. Maghrebi cuisine from Morocco, Tunisia, Algeria, and Libya is built around couscous and is known for spice blends such as harissa."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"arab-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arab_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":7,"links":[["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Kibbeh safarjaliyeh","dish/kibbeh-safarjaliyeh"],["Ma'amoul","dish/ma-amoul"],["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Shakshouka","dish/shakshouka"],["Shawarma","dish/shawarma"],["Torshi","dish/torshi"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Malaysian cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Tahini","ingredient/tahini"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Kibbeh","ingredient/kibbeh"],["Pomegranate","ingredient/pomegranate"],["Phoenix dactylifera","ingredient/phoenix-dactylifera"],["Pistachios","ingredient/pistachios"],["Walnuts","ingredient/walnuts"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Anise","ingredient/anise"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Caraway seed","ingredient/caraway-seed"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Pine nut","ingredient/pine-nut"],["Pistachio","ingredient/pistachio"],["Pistachio nut","ingredient/pistachio-nut"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Malaysian cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/c/c3/Petra_metzes.jpg/500px-Petra_metzes.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Unai Guerra","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petra_metzes.jpg"}},"cuisine/acehnese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/acehnese-cuisine","n":"Acehnese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Indonesia","intro":[["Acehnese cuisine is the cuisine of the Acehnese people of Aceh in Sumatra, Indonesia. This cuisine is popular and widely known in Indonesia. Arab, Persian, and Indian traders influenced food culture in Aceh although flavours have substantially changed their original forms. The spices combined in Acehnese cuisine are commonly found in Indian and Arab cuisine, such as ginger, pepper, coriander, cumin, cloves, cinnamon, cardamom, and fennel."],["A variety of Acehnese food is cooked with curry or coconut milk, which is generally combined with meat such as buffalo, beef, goat meat, lamb, mutton, fish, or chicken. Several Aceh dishes can trace their origins to India, such as roti canai, which was derived from Indian flatbread."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"acehnese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acehnese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":5,"links":[["Keumamah","dish/keumamah"],["Meuseukat","dish/meuseukat"],["Mie aceh","dish/mie-aceh"],["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"],["Timphan","dish/timphan"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","lin